-- Breakout — bricks, removal, and a wall that thins as you play.
--
-- What this one exercises that Pong does not:
--   * many objects of one class, laid out in a grid
--   * removal: `visible 0` makes a brick inert — not drawn, not collided with
--   * a win condition counted rather than reached
--   * real sprite-budget pressure (a brick wall is a lot of hardware sprites)

start title

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- title -----
--
-- The artwork is demade by the image engine at the screen's own size, into a
-- tilemap the background layer draws for nothing — the same fitter a photograph
-- goes through. What it costs is tiles, and the compiler says so when a picture
-- wants more than the game's own art has left.

scene title

backdrop breakout.title.svg

create text prompt in title (x centerx - 7, y screenheight - 2, text "press a to play")

when a pressed in title then scene as play


scene play

-- The playfield is demade too, at the screen's own size, by the same fitter the
-- title screen went through. It is scenery and nothing else: the game reads
-- nothing from it and collides with none of it — what it costs is tiles.
backdrop breakout.play.svg

-- The music and the effects go through the demakers the CLI's own `arrange` and
-- `sfx` commands use. A brick is the sound this game is *about*, so it is the
-- one with a noise component; the bounce is a plain blip and gives way to it.
music arcade.mid in play
sound brick.wav on ball hits brick
sound bounce.wav on ball hits paddle


create object ball (width 1 cell, height 1 cell, speed 45vmin, sprite ball.svg)
create object paddle (width 18vw, height 1 cell, speed 70vw, sprite paddle.svg)
create object brick (width 12vw, height 1 cell, sprite brick.svg)

create ball ball1 in play (x centerx, y centery + 2, direction northeast)
create paddle bat in play (x centerx, y screenheight - 2)

-- A counter with no caption is a number nobody can read. The label is an
-- ordinary `text` object beside it — same font, same layer, no new concept.
create text scoretag in play (x 1, y 1, text "score:")
create number score in play (value 0, x 8, y 1)
create text lefttag in play (x screenwidth - 9, y 1, text "left:")
create number left in play (value 8, x screenwidth - 3, y 1)

-- Two rows of four. Positions are relative, so the wall spans the same share of
-- every playfield even though a Game Boy brick is 2 cells and a Mega Drive one
-- is 5.
create brick b1 in play (x 4vw, y 3)
create brick b2 in play (x 28vw, y 3)
create brick b3 in play (x 52vw, y 3)
create brick b4 in play (x 76vw, y 3)
create brick b5 in play (x 4vw, y 5)
create brick b6 in play (x 28vw, y 5)
create brick b7 in play (x 52vw, y 5)
create brick b8 in play (x 76vw, y 5)

control bat left (xdirection -1) on hold
control bat right (xdirection 1) on hold

when bat hits screenleft, screenright then xdirection as 0

when ball hits screenleft, screenright then xdirection as flip
when ball hits screentop then ydirection as flip

-- Losing the ball costs nothing but the serve: this is a demo, not a punishment.
when ball hits screenbottom then (ball1.y, ball1.x, ball1.ydirection) as (centery + 2, centerx, -1)

when ball hits paddle then (ydirection, xdirection) as (flip, (ball.centerx - paddle.centerx) / paddle.width)

-- A brick that has been hit stops being drawn *and* stops being solid, so the
-- ball passes through where it used to be. One property, both meanings.
when ball hits brick then (ydirection, brick.visible, score.value, left.value) as (flip, 0, score.value + 1, left.value - 1)

when score.value reaches 8 in play then scene as cleared

scene cleared

create text won in cleared (x centerx - 4, y centery, text "wall down")

when a pressed in cleared then scene as play
